<h1>Ignore Patterns</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Gate implements a small custom glob parser. It is not a complete <code>.gitignore</code> implementation.</p>

<h2 id="file">The file</h2>
<p>Gate reads <code>.gateignore</code> only from the directory where it is run. Paths are compared relative to that directory. Blank lines and lines whose first non-space character is <code>#</code> are ignored.</p>
<div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Whole-file patterns</span>
node_modules/**
dist/**
fixtures/*.json

<span class="cmt"># Re-include a matching path</span>
!fixtures/security.json

<span class="cmt"># Suppress one rule only in matching files</span>
[rule:high-entropy-string] test/**</div>

<h2 id="glob">Implemented glob tokens</h2>
<table class="cmd-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Current meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>*</code></td><td>Zero or more characters other than <code>/</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>**/</code></td><td>Zero or more directory segments.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>**</code></td><td>Zero or more characters, including separators.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>?</code></td><td>Exactly one character other than <code>/</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>{js,ts}</code></td><td>Literal brace alternatives.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>trailing <code>/</code></td><td>The named directory and descendants.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Patterns are anchored to the whole relative path. A bare <code>config.js</code> matches the root file only; <code>**/config.js</code> also matches nested paths. Character classes such as <code>[0-9]</code>, Git's directory rules, escaping, and ordered last-match-wins semantics are not implemented.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Cross-platform caveat:</strong> matching is written around <code>/</code>, while relative paths come from Node's platform path API. Verify patterns on Windows instead of assuming slash normalization.</div>

<h2 id="negation">Negation does not follow Git order</h2>
<p>A leading <code>!</code> creates a negation pattern. Gate checks all negations before any positive file pattern. If any negation matches, the file is scanned, regardless of where the lines appear or which positive pattern also matches.</p>
<div class="code-block">fixtures/**
!fixtures/security.json</div>
<p>This re-includes <code>fixtures/security.json</code>. Moving the two lines does not change the result. That differs from <code>.gitignore</code>, where later matching rules normally win.</p>

<h2 id="rule">Rule-specific patterns</h2>
<div class="code-block">[rule:high-entropy-string] test/**
[rule:stripe-test-secret] fixtures/payments.js</div>
<p>The exact syntax is <code>[rule:&lt;rule-id&gt;] &lt;glob&gt;</code>. Gate still scans the file, then removes findings whose rule ID and relative path both match. There is no wildcard rule ID or severity expression.</p>

<h2 id="inline">Inline comments</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="kw">const</span> example = <span class="str">"fixture-value"</span>; <span class="cmt">// gate-ignore</span>
<span class="kw">const</span> demo = <span class="str">"fixture-value"</span>; <span class="cmt">// gate-ignore: test fixture</span>
<span class="kw">const</span> other = <span class="str">"fixture-value"</span>; <span class="cmt">/* gate-ignore: generated sample */</span></div>
<p>The marker must be a JavaScript-style <code>//</code> or <code>/* ... */</code> comment at the end of the physical line. It suppresses every ordinary line-based finding on that line; the optional reason is parsed but not added to an approval ledger.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code># gate-ignore</code> is not recognized.</li>
  <li>There is no <code>gate-ignore-next-line</code>, block disable/enable pair, or inline rule selector.</li>
  <li>The full-file multiline pre-pass does not consult inline markers, so a multiline finding may remain even when one constituent line has a marker.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="interactive">Interactive ignore action</h2>
<p>Choosing <code>i</code> in <code>gate scan --interactive</code> appends an exact rule-and-file entry to the repository's <code>.gateignore</code>:</p>
<div class="code-block">[rule:stripe-test-secret] test/fixture.js</div>
<p>Gate does not ask for an expiry, owner, or justification and does not deduplicate entries. Review the diff before committing it.</p>

<h2 id="order">Effective suppression order</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Negation and whole-file patterns decide whether the path is scanned.</li>
  <li>Inline end-of-line markers skip ordinary line scanning for that line.</li>
  <li>Rule-specific patterns remove matching findings after the scan.</li>
  <li>A <code>.gaterc</code> severity value of <code>ignore</code> removes the remaining findings for that rule.</li>
  <li>Other severity overrides change the label.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="testing">Test an ignore safely</h2>
<div class="code-block"><span class="prompt">$ </span><span class="cmd">gate scan path/to/fixture --format json</span></div>
<p>Run the exact path from the same working directory your hook or CI job uses. Check skipped/error information as well as findings. An ignore should suppress the narrowest intended rule and path, and the reason should be recorded in code review even though Gate does not enforce one.</p>

<div class="warning-box"><strong>Ignores are a policy bypass.</strong> Anyone who can change <code>.gateignore</code>, inline markers, or severity overrides can silence findings. Protect those files with ordinary review and ownership controls.</div>
